  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "$$\n{\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}4zy\\,dy]dA,\n$$\n\nEvaluate the repeated integral above given that $D$ is a region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z=0,$ $x=2$ and the line $z-x=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}4zy\\,dy]dA & ={\u222c}_{D}2z[{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}2zx\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate this double integral, we plot $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/assets/b748f589d7fa773444fc45bdbd1d94a5424bb81c40851f0f0f2542a4f66c0c8d.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}2zx\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2zx\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =4-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
